#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:146
#: 2e1973fb01324e69bc87e3194112c88b
msgid "Key parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:148
#: 1ad632e7774942d39adb636f0df3c165
msgid "The key differences between fixed wing flight and VTOL for a tailsitter are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:151
#: 280aa1870e3e4c989346c4a961980480
msgid "when in VTOL the copter style PID gains will be used (the ones starting with ``Q_A_RAT_x``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:153
#: 3248789098e04860bece03a3679c48fc
msgid "when in fixed wing flight the fixed wing PID gains will be used (the ``RLL_RATE_x`` and ``PTCH_RATE_x`` gains)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:155
#: 448b09dcf47e491e889f2e8a3b1d52fc
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX<Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX>` allows the roll limit angle limit to be set differently from :ref:`Q_A_ANGLE_MAX<Q_A_ANGLE_MAX>`. If left at zero, both pitch and roll are limited by :ref:`Q_A_ANGLE_MAX<Q_A_ANGLE_MAX>`. If :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX<Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX>` is nonzero roll angle will be limited and pitch max angle will still be :ref:`Q_A_ANGLE_MAX<Q_A_ANGLE_MAX>`. This should be set if your tailsitter can achieve much larger pitch angle than would be safe for roll (some airframes can't recover from high-speed knife-edge flight using only yaw control)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:156
#: b67c0844e5ed4b6aa807b5d5b44741cd
msgid ":ref:`Q_TRIM_PITCH<Q_TRIM_PITCH>` can be used to account for any offset in hovering pitch angle due to the thrust line not being through the CG, or to counter fixed wing AHRS trim used to set angle of attack in cruise, instead of using :ref:`PTCH_TRIM_DEG<PTCH_TRIM_DEG>` for adjusting the trim since it is only in fixed modes and AHRS trim affects all modes. Set this such that the vehicle does not drift forwards or backwards in QSTABILIZE or QHOVER with no pilot inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:158
#: 4c6b1fb5720f452e831cd4a21722d338
msgid "After calibrating the accelerometers, using the \"LEVEL\" calibration position as normal fixed wing flying attitude, you may find that the VTOL nose up hover drifts to one side (ie earth frame roll), as well as front to back. While there is a :ref:`Q_TRIM_PITCH<Q_TRIM_PITCH>` adjustment for pitch trim, there is not for roll. You may use the \"LEVEL\" only calibrate button in Mission Planner (the \"ahrstrim\" command in MAVProxy) to set the pitch and roll trim while in Nose Up VTOL hover attitude, if you change the mode to QSTABILIZE or QHOVER while doing so. This may change the fixed wing pitch trim, but not the fixed wing roll trim. Therefore, you may have to re-adjust the :ref:`PTCH_TRIM_DEG<PTCH_TRIM_DEG>` parameter to get the desired pitch attitude in fixed wing flight, as well as the :ref:`Q_TRIM_PITCH<Q_TRIM_PITCH>` value, but this should correct the side drift."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:161
#: b81aa12855ab453da4ffafc6eb70e0d2
msgid "Vectored Thrust"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:163
#: 2438587bf78b4ac08f97936f816c1749
msgid "If your tailsitter has vectored thrust then you should set the ``SERVOn_FUNCTION`` values for your two tilt servos for the left and right motors and for the left and right motor throttles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:167
#: 3dd31664c5384590bc503d371681fea0
msgid "All tailsitters ignore the :ref:`Q_TILT_TYPE<Q_TILT_TYPE>` parameter, and require continuous output servos, and will drive the tilt servos appropriately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:169
#: 2bc96312caf64bad991a13c5300e1151
msgid "For example, if your left tilt servo is channel 5 and your right tilt servo is channel 6, then set:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:172
#: 72f19e05a7194ad0a3c13e88b42d8486
msgid ":ref:`SERVO5_FUNCTION<SERVO5_FUNCTION>` =75"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:173
#: 4dff3f4f886d471f9f63cfb8e9f85ccf
msgid ":ref:`SERVO6_FUNCTION<SERVO6_FUNCTION>` =76"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:175
#: 4ab5ab90f47a4dfb84ea2efd2f3292f8
msgid "and you need to assign left throttle to the left motor and right throttle to the right motor, for example using the SERVO 7 and SERVO 8 outputs, for left and right motor escs, respectively:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:177
#: e7e2b25f1bf24ac699395c94e1f296a2
msgid ":ref:`SERVO7_FUNCTION<SERVO7_FUNCTION>` =73"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:178
#: 2958fbffc9104ed0804fd8ddb2dff310
msgid ":ref:`SERVO8_FUNCTION<SERVO8_FUNCTION>` =74"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:180
#: 4384bb937a6041218f8a7557ee29cba2
msgid "You also need to set the right ``SERVOn_REVERSED`` values, and the correct ``SERVOn_TRIM``, ``SERVOn_MIN`` and ``SERVOn_MAX`` values, as appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:183
#: a15dff31dd99469c94f9cc7197d60428
msgid ":ref:`Q_A_ANGLE_BOOST<Q_A_ANGLE_BOOST>` should be disabled for vectored thrust tailsitters. Failure to disable this will cause the throttle to decrease as the nose dips, making the nose dip even further and resulting in a crash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:185
#: d64f2b678ade46b199a18a363ff2ed99
msgid "When disarmed, switching to QHOVER or QLOITER will force the motors forward into fixed wing orientation. If armed in this position, a prop strike could occur for Belly Sitter configurations. Tilt will be raised to VTOL position when throttle is raised above idle, but the strike will have already occurred. The solution is to momentarily raise the throttle above idle, allowing the tilts to raise, return throttle stick to idle, then arm. This needs to be done also for AUTO mode takeoffs, which should be started from QSTABILIZE with motors raised, armed, and then change to AUTO for the takeoff."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:188
#: 212d99e013034c2093c46bce4141477b
msgid "Tilt Rotor Movement Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:189
#: 707b952e7da24bf0835252b98f446cab
msgid "See :ref:`Tilt Rotor Setup Tips<tilt-rotor-tips>` and :ref:`Tilt Rotor Servo Setup<tilt-rotor-setup>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:194
#: 6dba2c02e5514c6e8fb02a0cc878bfef
msgid "TVBS (Thrust Vectored Belly Sitter)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:196
#: 1fcccb4a2978488695043a3f9ff7dcda
msgid "TVBS are just dual motor vectored thrust tailsitters that don't sit on their tails, but rather their belly. Aside from making sure props clear (ie sufficient throw on tilt servos) when in the horizontal stance, nothing special is required to make it take off from a horizontal stance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:198
#: 9374a188b0a94931a432f6857ad33b5d
msgid "At least 45 degree throw, either side of neutral (fixed wing flight position) is required, with 60 degrees being most desirable. Otherwise, the vehicle will \"skid\" along the ground a bit when you raise the throttle to bring it vertical on takeoff. Takeoffs, required a decisive move of throttle to hover or above to reduce the \"skid\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:202
#: b419ded707f84947b0da31fbae1a34db
msgid "For landing in fixed wing, manual throttle controlled modes, there is an ``RCx_OPTION`` (89) that will force the tilt servos upright at idle throttle, and optionally force the pitch to target :ref:`LAND_PITCH_DEG<LAND_PITCH_DEG>` for flaring to the normal fixed wing landing. This allows intentional or emergency fixed wing landings in MANUAL, ACRO, STABILIZE, and FBWA modes without the risk of a prop strike in configurations where this could occur otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:205
#: 8422b25461a84cffa6e402332dd04c01
msgid "Vectored Gains"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:207
#: cafbb8d2578e47058c2c014395d29ed5
msgid "There are two vectoring gains available. One controls the amount of vectored thrust movement in hover, and the other controls the amount of vectored thrust movement in forward flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:211
#: b07391cd84534b71a227942963a4979a
msgid "The :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_VHGAIN<Q_TAILSIT_VHGAIN>` parameter controls vectored thrust in hover. A typical value is around 0.8, which gives a lot of control to vectored thrust in hover. This control is combined with control from your elevon mixing gain (controlled by :ref:`MIXING_GAIN<MIXING_GAIN>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:216
#: 9fc7153fe6324d5a9a6f59cd98e6fdbd
msgid "The :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_VFGAIN<Q_TAILSIT_VFGAIN>` parameter controls vectored thrust in forward flight. A typical value is around 0.2, which gives a small amount of control to vectored thrust in forward flight. This control is combined with control from your elevon mixing gain (controlled by :ref:`MIXING_GAIN<MIXING_GAIN>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:221
#: 819c6543b7ae4bb6a8ad7a88ddc6f735
msgid "By adjusting the relative values of :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_VHGAIN<Q_TAILSIT_VHGAIN>`, :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_VFGAIN<Q_TAILSIT_VFGAIN>` and :ref:`MIXING_GAIN<MIXING_GAIN>` you can adjust how much control you have from elevons and thrust vectoring in each flight mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:229
#: 40ca145802bd4f37af1f2a71e245b560
msgid "CopterMotor PID Gain Scaling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:231
#: 7beb393ba4a848c28e5c69fb7ee65630
msgid "There are a number of options for scaling control surface movement versus speed. Control surface effectiveness is dependent on airspeed they see, in tailsitter configurations this is heavily driven by the motor layout and prop wash. A copter tailsitter with lots of control authority from thrust and small control surfaces will be much less sensitive to these gain scheduling parameters. For a vehicle with large control surfaces care must be taken to setup gain scaling to best suit the configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:234
#: eea8d4c780a8452f80cd901361b7d9de
msgid "The gain scaling scheme is selected with :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_GSCMSK<Q_TAILSIT_GSCMSK>`, it is a bitmask, some options can be used in combination, others must be used in isolation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:236
#: 789debdd2abf4253982f735a51204804
msgid "The maximum and minimum scaling that can be applied by any scheme is set by :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_GSCMIN<Q_TAILSIT_GSCMIN>` and :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_GSCMAX<Q_TAILSIT_GSCMAX>`. If a scheme is working well at all but the extremes these endpoints can be adjusted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:238
#: 60da0c16c92b4609a47ed2741a366250
msgid "Scaling is done relative the hover throttle point, ensure this is set correctly before proceeding, see: :ref:`Flight Modes<quadplane-flight-modes>`, QHOVER mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:240
#: 71acaf6d85b547009a1c068e4785d2e5
msgid "Disk theory gain scaling is the most advanced method available and should result in the best results, if setup correctly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:243
#: b03c6db6cf2349ce8efbb203441b87cb
msgid "Throttle scaling (Bit 0)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:245
#: 9e906fbf1d944f48909fd697ba748fa9
msgid "Control surfaces will be scaled directly with throttle. High throttle will result in less control surface movement, low throttle will result in more. This method is always used for thrust vectoring scaling in non-CopterMotor tailsitters independent of :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_GSCMSK<Q_TAILSIT_GSCMSK>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:248
#: 6e2fff7e550d46e7a75fc8ebd2666724
msgid "Reduce gain at high throttle/tilt (Bit 1)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:250
#: 70a3ae257d8b40dea7b5ca930ce4dc69
msgid "Attitude/throttle based gain attenuation, with this option control surface deflection is reduced at high tilt angles and high throttle levels to prevent oscillation at high airspeeds. This can be used in combination with throttle scaling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:253
#: 821e981496924bc38562e289c1d579be
msgid "Disk theory (Bit 2)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:254
#: 410b8d245c344f2fa0fa42fce3d55bb9
msgid "Disk theory gain scaling attempts to calculate the airspeed seen on the control surfaces as a result of both prop wash and forward airspeed. In order for this calculation to be done :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_DSKLD<Q_TAILSIT_DSKLD>` must be set. This is the aircraft weight in KG divided by the total disk area of the propellers. The disk area for each propeller is calculated from the radius, the disk area of all propellers should then be summed. This allows ArduPilot to calculate the airspeed directly behind the propeller, however on a real vehicle 100% of the control surface is not in the direct prop wash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:257
#: a63d98478a9444a4bf45fdd744699d9f
msgid "For example if half of the control surfaces are in the prop wash the calculated disk loading value should also be halved. Some tuning will be required for best performance, If oscillations are seen at high throttle :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_DSKLD<Q_TAILSIT_DSKLD>` should be reduced."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:259
#: 466172725ff14030be6dfa9143621fc6
msgid "For best results an airspeed sensor should be fitted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:264
#: 47c55e1df4aa43d6baa5f4b0ef00e070
msgid "Altitude correction (Bit 3)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:266
#: f95dc73eb7c4469e91fe9dd0ee13c078
msgid "Gain is scaled with altitude, this should be considered when operating over a wide range of altitudes, this method can be enabled in combination with any other method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:269
#: dfa0f1e6cf5b446b934403424985dffe
msgid "Transitions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:271
#: ac49903be873483b9cf6d8b526374043
msgid "Tailsitter transitions are a little different than other QuadPlane transitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:273
#: 0a9509a657384eb7a8320da33326f568
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_ANGLE<Q_TAILSIT_ANGLE>` specifies how far the nose must pitch down in a VTOL mode before transition to forward flight is complete. So a value of e.g. 60 degrees results in switching from copter to plane controller (forward transition) when the nose reaches 30 degrees above the horizon (60 degrees down from vertical). The pitch rate used when pitching down to forward flight is given by :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_RAT_FW<Q_TAILSIT_RAT_FW>`, this rate will be held until :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_ANGLE<Q_TAILSIT_ANGLE>` is reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:276
#: d38be6c0a1e84f81b6559b3b1d1c3908
msgid "For the back transition from forward flight to VTOL, the plane controller will be used until the nose reaches :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_ANG_VT<Q_TAILSIT_ANG_VT>` above the horizon. If :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_ANG_VT<Q_TAILSIT_ANG_VT>` is 0 :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_ANGLE<Q_TAILSIT_ANGLE>` will be used for both forward and back transitions. The pitch rate used when pitching up to VTOL flight is given by :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_RAT_VT<Q_TAILSIT_RAT_VT>`, this rate will be held until :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_ANG_VT<Q_TAILSIT_ANG_VT>` is reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:279
#: 6d8539cd5e01491f91d65b014b596cc1
msgid "if you back transition while on the ground, ie sitting in a FW mode at zero throttle, armed, and then switch to a VTOL mode, the motors will immediately start spinning until the \"transition\" completes and the motors tilt up. This can result in ground movement. To help prevent this, you can set :ref:`Q_OPTIONS<Q_OPTIONS>` bit 18 to prevent arming unless already in a VTOL mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:281
#: 90d46776b8ca4200a03b89b6ed7116a7
msgid "Depending on the entry speed and time required to transition, the vehicle may gain altitude, sometimes significantly, since the throttle is set to the current :ref:`Q_M_THRST_HOVER<Q_M_THST_HOVER>` hover thrust value throughout the transition to VTOL. This can be overridden with a lower value by setting :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_THR_VT<Q_TAILSIT_THR_VT>`. With experimentation, changing the rates, angle, and this parameter for fixed wing to VTOL transitions, it is possible to obtain almost level altitude transitions. Especially with copter style tailsitters with no control surfaces using Q_TAILSIT_ENABLE = 2, keeping attitude control active even at low or zero throttle values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:283
#: 9fdddd267fc44ce49988cac6816569e9
msgid "During transitions, pilot input is disabled and vehicle attitude and throttle is controlled totally by the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:285
#: 138d9ddc089b467da868feee2d1a706a
msgid "ArduPilot will send as message to the GCS when transition is complete, these can be found in the Mission Planner messages tab. If the transition does not complete normally the transition parameters and vehicle tune should be checked. A example message is ```Transition FW done, timeout```, the timeout time is one and a half times the expected transition time as calculated from the angle and rate parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:288
#: e0fb2bd1ee844f1dad34c52aaf8491de
msgid "Control Surfaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:290
#: 67349ef17a0e405aa87200ffd55f31b8
msgid "Although usually not recommended, it is possible to fly a tailsitter aircraft with no control surfaces. Care should be taken to get the vehicle flying well in the hover modes first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:292
#: 0991cbaaa73f46ca9c72b959328527e3
msgid ":ref:`Q_OPTIONS<Q_OPTIONS>` bit 7:Force Qassist will force the vehicle to use the copter controller in all flight modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:293
#: 6f6cc53f079949ffab28825334d0d7b6
msgid ":ref:`Q_OPTIONS<Q_OPTIONS>` bit 8:Mtrs_Only_Qassist allows use of the copter controller for the motors but leave any controls surfaces under plane control, this allows the control surfaces to act as 'trim tabs' for the motors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:295
#: 753c4e0dbfd64db29d6dc2292c6500c1
msgid "See :ref:`Assisted Fixed-Wing Flight<assisted_fixed_wing_flight>` for more details on how Qassist can be automatically enabled and disabled with airspeed, altitude, attitude, and/or by ``RCx_OPTION`` switch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:298
#: ba70a72603f94a4d81401712ec378903
msgid "Tailsitter Input"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:300
#: 2711beb27560439ebb785f33cdc4e5cb
msgid "You can change how control inputs while hovering a tailsitter will be interpreted using the :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_INPUT<Q_TAILSIT_INPUT>` parameter. The choices are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:303
#: 7f715ea93f834599a8c05eb1982fc658
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_INPUT<Q_TAILSIT_INPUT>` =0 means that in hover the aircraft responds like a multi-rotor, with the yaw stick controlling earth-frame yaw, and roll stick controlling earth-frame roll. This is a good choice for pilots who are used to flying multi-rotor aircraft."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:308
#: 81553f60aabb47219b1339708ea3c65f
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_INPUT<Q_TAILSIT_INPUT>` =1 means that in hover the aircraft responds like a 3D aircraft, with the yaw stick controlling earth-frame roll, and roll stick controlling earth-frame yaw. This is a good choice for pilots who are used to flying 3D aircraft in prop-hang, but is not very useful when flying around, due to the earth-frame multicopter control inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:314
#: 4c237bd2c258461186f3d03dd1e5357e
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_INPUT<Q_TAILSIT_INPUT>` =2 and 3 mean that the aircraft responds like a 3D aircraft with the yaw stick controlling earth-frame yaw and the roll stick controlling body-frame roll when flying level. When hovering, these options behave the same as types 0 and 1, respectively. This is accomplished by splitting the roll and yaw command inputs into bodyframe roll and yaw components as a function of Euler pitch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:320
#: cec0ffa6dcf14f9d9df8f92e1f8719cf
msgid "Due to the rotation of the tailsitter body frame with respect to the multicopter body frame, the roll limits are set by parameter :ref:`Q_YAW_RATE_MAX<Q_YAW_RATE_MAX>` (in degrees), and the yaw rate limits are set by parameter :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX<Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX>` (in deg/sec).  The pitch limit is set by parameter :ref:`Q_A_ANGLE_MAX<Q_A_ANGLE_MAX>` (in degrees), and this also serves as the yaw rate limit if :ref:`Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX<Q_TAILSIT_RLL_MX>` is zero. If any rate limit is too high for the airframe, you may experience glitches in attitude control at high rates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:322
#: 5b7f55cfe9d243aa82d956899e6e5201
msgid ":ref:`Q_TAILSIT_INPUT<Q_TAILSIT_INPUT>` is ignored in QACRO modes. All inputs are body-frame referenced."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:325
#: 30c10953915a4b148e203a033a2b51ea
msgid "Tailsitter Input Mask"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:327
#: a45140495e544d8aa048322e5437f955
msgid "Use of this feature is not recommended since its removed in 4.2 and  later firmware revisions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:330
#: de36820454654d31a45dce760a29d2e2
msgid "To support people with experience flying 3D aircraft and wanting to learn how to prop-hang manually, you can set the ``Q_TAILSIT_MASK<Q_TAILSIT_MASK>`` to determine which channels will have full manual input control without attitude stabilization while hovering in QHOVER and QSTABILIZE."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:334
#: 1690979301df4b9ba83b9478f3543376
msgid "The mask of manual channels is enabled using a transmitter input channel, specified with the ``Q_TAILSIT_MASKCH<Q_TAILSIT_MASKCH>`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:337
#: f4ae858eaffc427e955a9a84ba6c787f
msgid "For example, if you are learning how to fly 3D aircraft, and you want some assistance learning how to best control the rudder, then you can set:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:341
#: d96efb873d1c477799ad379f74686f2f
msgid "``Q_TAILSIT_MASK<Q_TAILSIT_MASK>`` =8 (for rudder)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:342
#: 09595e9886df4a8881e5f451074adeac
msgid "``Q_TAILSIT_MASKCH<Q_TAILSIT_MASKCH>`` =7"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:344
#: 70d679df848349d581e0ae1878b9a261
msgid "then when channel 7 goes above 1700 the pilot will be given full manual control of rudder when hovering. This provides good 3D piloting practice on one or more axes at a time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:349
#: b86d73195d9a4ea891e895a04e58fc18
msgid "Center of Gravity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:351
#: c60b808430d043c9a3e492e402c3759e
msgid "The center of gravity for a tailsitter is important in an extra dimension. When hovering it is important that there is not too much weight in the belly of the plane or on its back, so that it leans forward or back. This is particularly important for non-vectored tailsitters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:358
#: 6cf2f2184b0243aa98e862198e53a1d4
msgid "Pre-Arm Issues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:360
#: fec71faa656a48078123f5323b56a7d6
msgid "Due to an issue in DCM related to compass fusion for yaw when pointing straight up, sometimes the AHRS subsystems will disagree when powering up, nose up. Slight errors in compass calibration, while resulting in a successful calibration, may worsen this effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:362
#: 2666d57c8efc468ebd5d2b21f35d89f6
msgid "The result is that some setups will give a pre-arm failure. Typically it is \"Pre-Arm:DCM roll/pitch inconsistent by \"x\" degrees\" or similar. If this happens consistently, then one of two solutions can be used:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:364
#: 76cfb2a254744a42bb1252ce6e47ac4e
msgid "Power up horizontally, and allow the autopilot to begin initialization in this position. After the IMUs tilt initialization is completed (usually in the first ten to fifteen seconds or so), the Tailsitter can be set vertically for the remainder of the initialization (ie after GPS lock and EKF is using the GPS) and then armed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/guide-tailsitter.rst:365
#: 70f8e7fda3f44f07a515bc247170852f
msgid "Or, if you get the Pre-Arm failure above, lay the Tailsitter down horizontally for 10-30 seconds to allow the various AHRS subsystems to synchronize. After that it can be raised and arming should proceed normally."
msgstr ""
